{
  "before": [
    {
      "text": "MACROECONOMICS",
      "size": 23.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "MACRO-42",
      "size": 13.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Time:",
      "size": 8.5,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "6 minutes",
      "size": 8.5,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Outcome:",
      "size": 8.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Calculate private, public, and national saving in",
      "size": 7.25,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "closed and open economies.",
      "size": 7.25,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Difficulty:",
      "size": 8.5,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Intermediate",
      "size": 8.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Saving, Investment, and National-Saving Identities",
      "size": 17.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "+",
      "size": 15.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "THE CORE IDEA",
      "size": 14.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "With net taxes T, private saving is Y - T - C and public saving is T - G. National saving is their sum: S = Y - C - G. In a closed economy, S",
      "size": 8.15,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "= I. In an open economy, S = I + NCO; because NX = NCO in the course model, S = I + NX. These are aggregate identities, not claims",
      "size": 8.15,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "that each saver directly funds one investor.",
      "size": 8.15,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "?",
      "size": 15.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "HOW TO RECOGNIZE IT",
      "size": 14.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "-",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Budget surplus means T - G is positive; a deficit makes public saving negative.",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "-",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adding private and public saving cancels T and gives Y - C - G.",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "-",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Use S = I only for the closed-economy model.",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "-",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "In an open economy, saving can finance domestic investment or net capital outflow.",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "!",
      "size": 15.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "WATCH OUT",
      "size": 13.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Do not reverse the public-saving sign. T - G is public saving; G - T is the budget deficit. Also state the closed-economy assumption",
      "size": 8.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "before writing S = I.",
      "size": 8.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "#",
      "size": 15.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE: FROM COMPONENTS TO OPEN-ECONOMY SAVING",
      "size": 12.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "KEY RELATIONSHIPS",
      "size": 9.5,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Private saving = Y - T - C",
      "size": 7.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Public saving = T - G",
      "size": 7.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "National saving = Y - C - G",
      "size": 7.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Closed: S = I",
      "size": 7.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Open: S = I + NCO; NX = NCO",
      "size": 7.2,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "Let Y = $1,200, T = $150, C = $760, and G = $260. Private saving is $290;",
      "size": 8.45,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "public saving is -$110; national saving is $180. If domestic investment is",
      "size": 8.45,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "$140, then NCO = S - I = $40. The accounting identity also gives NX = $40.",
      "size": 8.45,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "?",
      "size": 15.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "CHECK YOURSELF",
      "size": 13.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Private saving is $220 and public saving is -$50. What is national saving?",
      "size": 8.35,
      "font": "/Helvetica"
    },
    {
      "text": "READY?",
      "size": 15.0,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    },
    {
      "text": "Return to the game and master this concept.",
      "size": 10.5,
      "font": "/Helvetica-Bold"
    }
  ],
